  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 AM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nCurrently, broad upper troughing is deepening across the eastern \nU.S. with a surface low near 1,000 mb progressing east from the Ohio \nRiver Valley. Its associated cold front is moving in from the west, \nhaving produced earlier showers and storms. This will lead to a \nshift towards northwesterly flow through the day and the beginning \nof CAA after the front is east of the region. With notably less \noverall moisture, the focus for showers will be along the higher \nterrain due to orographic lifting. Generally 20 to 25 kts of \nnorthwesterly 850mb flow is anticipated with temperatures near and \nbelow this level remaining above freezing for the day, keeping all \nrain. Shortly after sunset, however, 850mb temperatures will fall \nbelow freezing with another reinforcing shortwave arriving from the \nnorth. During this time, plumes of moisture will approach from the \nsame are after fetch from Lake Michigan. This will lead to \nincreasing PoPs into Friday morning, mainly along the higher \nterrain. Additional plumes of moisture rotate through by later on \nFriday into Friday night with better potential for light rain or \nsnow in northern Valley locations. Based on the latest guidance, \ntemperatures at and below 925mb stay above freezing until late into \nFriday evening, but evaporative cooling could lead to more \nchangeover. By this time, moisture quickly becomes more limited \nabove 850mb, especially into Saturday morning. \n\nBased on the overall setup and indications from high-res model \nguidance, higher elevation places in East Tennessee and southwest \nVirginia are very likely to see accumulating snow. Anywhere from 1 \nto 4 inches seems reasonable at or above 3,000 feet in elevation \nwith locally higher amounts across the highest peaks. Some other \nlocations around or above 2,000 feet could see accumulations as well \nbut probably more confined to grassy surfaces. For places lower than \n2,000 feet, flurries and light snow showers are certainly possible \nby Friday evening into Friday night. Beyond the snowfall, strong CAA \nand persistent northwesterly flow will make for notably cold wind \nchills across the higher elevations into the teens by Friday night \ninto Saturday. High pressure then builds in to the region on \nSaturday, leading to increasing subsidence and significantly colder \nlow temperatures into Sunday morning. Most places are likely to drop \nwell into the 20s. \n\nHigh pressure remains in place on Sunday with troughing having \npushed off to the east. This will promote recovery of high \ntemperatures into the 50s for most locations. Heading into the next \nweek, high pressure will remain nearly stationary with gradual \nheight rises continuing the dry and warming trend with most places \nback into the 60s by mid-week.",
